# Component conventions

## Server vs client

Server Components are the default. `layout`, `page`, `About`, `Work`, `Oss`, `Builds`,
`Contact`, and `Footer` all stay on the server. Add `"use client"` only for state,
effects, or browser APIs — that's `NavBar`, `SidebarNav`, `Hero`, `Impact`,
`Experience`, `Reveal`, and the two canvas components.

When a static section needs an animation, pass its server-rendered children into
`<Reveal>` rather than marking the section `"use client"`. `About` is the reference: a
Server Component whose paragraphs are rendered on the server and handed to a client
wrapper as a `children` slot.

## Motion

Use the shared `Reveal` component for scroll entrances instead of writing Framer Motion
props inline. Stagger with an index-scaled delay, and use `as` so the wrapper doesn't
break semantics:

```tsx
{items.map((item, i) => (
  <Reveal key={item.id} delay={0.06 * (i + 1)} as="article">…</Reveal>
))}
```

**Every animation needs a reduced-motion path.** In React code call `useReducedMotion()`
from Framer Motion and render the final state — `Hero` skips the typing animation,
`Impact` skips the count-up, `Experience` drops the `layoutId` indicator, `Reveal`
returns a plain `<div>`. This is a hard requirement; all existing animations honor it.

Timings: 0.2–0.3s for interaction, ~0.55s for entrances, mostly ease-out.

## Styling

Use the shared classes from `globals.css` before writing new CSS: `.section`,
`.section-title` (injects the teal `/` and trailing rule via pseudo-elements),
`.card-lift`, `.btn-accent`, `.btn-ghost`, `.skill-bullet`, `.intro-cursor`.

Colors come from Tailwind utilities generated by the `@theme inline` block —
`bg-navy`, `bg-light-navy`, `border-lightest-navy`, `text-slate`, `text-light-slate`,
`text-lightest-slate`, `text-accent`. Note the near-white token is `white-soft`, not
`white`. `--accent-tint` and `--navy-shadow` have no utility class; reference them with
`var()` inside arbitrary values. No raw hex in components.

Standard card: `card-lift rounded border border-lightest-navy bg-light-navy p-5`.

Monospace (`font-mono`) is the technical voice — labels, numbers, tech stacks, buttons,
nav. Sans is for prose. Section headings are lowercase.

Use `cn()` from `@/lib/utils` for conditional classes so `tailwind-merge` resolves
conflicts correctly.

## Accessibility

- `aria-hidden` on decoration: glow divs, gradient blobs, the blink cursor, icons beside
  a text label.
- `aria-label` on icon-only links.
- `aria-labelledby` on sections, pointing at their `<h2>` id.
- `target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"` on every external link.
- The `Experience` tabs implement the full ARIA tabs pattern with roving `tabIndex` —
  preserve it if you touch them.

## Miscellany

- Named exports (`export function About()`); default exports only in `layout.tsx` and
  `page.tsx`.
- Import via `@/`, prefer `@/components/ui/icons` over another inline SVG copy.
- Raw `<img>` for `/profile.jpg` is intentional — the file is pixel-sampled by
  `AsciiPortrait` and can't go through the optimizer. The
  `@next/next/no-img-element` disables are deliberate. Use `next/image` for new images
  that don't touch a canvas.
- Adding a section: component → `id` + `className="section"` → copy in `site.ts` →
  render in `page.tsx` → **add `{ id, label }` to `nav`**. The last step is the one that
  gets missed, and the `id` must match exactly or the anchor and scroll-spy break.
- `Reveal` returns a plain `<div>` under reduced motion regardless of `as`, so
  `<Reveal as="li">` inside a `<ul>` produces technically invalid HTML. Known issue.
